WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.771
A square root reverses squaring, with one important convention: the square-root symbol chooses the nonnegative answer

00:00:06.771 --> 00:00:15.133
A square with twenty five unit cells has side length five, so the principal square root of twenty five is five.

00:00:16.433 --> 00:00:19.646
Solving a squared equation asks a different question

00:00:19.646 --> 00:00:24.064
Both positive five and negative five produce twenty five when squared

00:00:24.064 --> 00:00:32.900
The graph crosses the height twenty five twice, so the equation has two real solutions although the principal root has one value.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.331
To simplify a radical, extract a perfect-square factor

00:00:37.331 --> 00:00:43.986
Eighteen is nine times two, so its square root is three times the square root of two

00:00:43.986 --> 00:00:47.900
Splitting a product this way is valid for nonnegative factors.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.737
The principal root must stay nonnegative even when its input was formed by squaring a negative number

00:00:55.737 --> 00:01:02.275
Therefore the square root of x squared is the absolute value of x, not always x itself

00:01:02.275 --> 00:01:04.967
Negative radicands have no real square root.
